hypotheses.statement:
The function $B$ extends holomorphically to the upper half-plane and across the positive real axis, is real and strictly decreasing there, and satisfies $B(1)=1$, $B'(1)=-1$. There are a constant $c\ge0$ and a positive measure $\nu$ on $[0,\infty)$ such that $$\label{int:stieltjes-form}
 -B'(s)=c+\int_{[0,\infty)}\frac{d\nu(r)}{s+r}
 \quad(s>0),\qquad
 \int_{[0,\infty)}\frac{d\nu(r)}{1+r}<\infty.$$ In particular $-B'(s)>0$ for every $s>0$.

For $i=1,2$, the slice map on the centered output space satisfies $$\label{eq:hessian}
 \|L_iZ\|_i^2
 \le\frac{1+\varepsilon}{1-\kappa}\,\|Z\|_0^2.$$ It consequently extends continuously to the completed centered spaces. For bounded observables this extension agrees with <a href="#eq:slice-map" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:slice-map">[eq:slice-map]</a>.

The positive weights $$\label{eq:thermal-metric}
 F_s(v)=\frac1{h_s}
 \frac{f(h_sv/2)f(-h_s/2)}{f(h_s(v-1)/2)}
 =\frac{b_s(v)-(r_s+\tfrac12)v}{1-v}$$ satisfy <a href="#eq:weight-comparison" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:weight-comparison">[eq:weight-comparison]</a>. The quotient at $v=1$ is understood continuously. For each fixed $r_s>0$ there are constants $0<c_s\le C_s<\infty$, independent of the minimizing states, such that $c_s\le F_s(v)\le C_s$ for every $v\in\mathbb R$.

At each minimizing state, $q_s$ has finite dual norm. Set $$K_{s,lr}=(r_s+1)p_r-r_sp_l.$$ Then $$\label{eq:dual-norm-sum}
 \|q_s\|_{F_s,*}^2
 =\sum_{j,l,r}(1-v_s(l,r))K_{s,lr}|(d'_{s,j})_{lr}|^2.$$ The sums obtained by replacing the coefficient on the right by $|K_{s,lr}|$ or $|v_s(l,r)K_{s,lr}|$ are finite.

Set $$A_*^2=\sum_iw_i\|q_i\|_{F_i,*}^2,
 \qquad M_*^2=\sum_iw_i|\mu_i|^2,
 \qquad C_0=(h_0\inf_{v\in\mathbb R}F_0(v))^{-1/2}.$$ Then $$\label{eq:defect-triangle}
 \|q_0\|_{F_0,*}\le A_*+\sqrt{2\kappa}\,C_0M_*.$$ In particular, the previously reserved choice of $\kappa$ may be made small enough that $$\label{eq:defect-estimate}
 \|q_0\|_{F_0,*}^2
 \le A_*^2+\frac\varepsilon2(A_*^2+M_*^2)$$ holds uniformly in $\zeta$ and the minimizing states.

At a minimizing pair, $\delta_s$ is traceless and $W_s^2\delta_sW_s^2$ is trace class. If $\Phi_i$ is the raw channel with the other two input states fixed, extended linearly to trace-class operators, then $$\label{eq:generator-covariance}
 \delta_0=(1-\kappa)\sum_i\Phi_i(\delta_i).$$ The traces against the forms $H_s$ and $W_s^4$ are well defined, and $$\label{eq:logarithmic-slicing}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\delta_0H_0=(1-\kappa)\sum_i\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\delta_iT_iH_0.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Let $n\ge1$ be finite, let $s,N_B\ge0$ be finite, and let $0\le\eta\le1$. Every state $\rho$ on $n$ modes with $\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\rho\sum_{j=1}^n a_j^\dagger a_j<\infty$ satisfies $$\label{eq:thermal-attenuator-bound}
 \frac1n S\!\left(\mathcal E_{\eta,N_B}^{\otimes n}(\rho)\right)
 \ge g\!\left(\eta g^{-1}\!\left(\frac{S(\rho)}n\right)
                   +(1-\eta)N_B\right).$$ In particular, the exact constrained minimum is $$\label{eq:thermal-attenuator-minimum}
 \min_{\substack{\rho\text{ a state on }n\text{ modes}\\
                  \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\rho\sum_{j=1}^n a_j^\dagger a_j<\infty\\
                  S(\rho)=ns}}
 \frac1n S\!\left(\mathcal E_{\eta,N_B}^{\otimes n}(\rho)\right)
 =g\!\left(\eta g^{-1}(s)+(1-\eta)N_B\right).$$ It is attained by the product thermal input $\tau_{g^{-1}(s)}$. No independence assumption is imposed among the modes of $\rho$; only the environment is required to be independent of that input.

At every coordinate of every space, let $m>0$ and $x,y\in\mathbb R$ be given. Suppose each $Z\in\mathcal T$ is supported on finitely many values of the parameter tuple $(m,x,y)$, and $\mathcal T$ is invariant under scalar multiplication by functions of this tuple. If the four families $$\label{int:four-weights}
 mf(x)e^x,\quad mf(x)e^{-x},\quad mf(y)e^y,\quad mf(y)e^{-y}$$ satisfy comparison, then so does $$\label{int:target-weight}
 F=m\frac{f(x)f(y)}{f(x+y)}.$$

